/cost now charges models.dev's current prices for three models users can pick. The price audit against models.dev went from 66 mismatched rows to 62, and no row that matched before stopped matching. Build, tests, clippy and fmt all pass. I made two commits (f4c1e96c and 417e6013) and haven't pushed them.

Prices changed (per million tokens: input / cache read / output):

| Model | Before | Now |
|---|---|---|
| mistral-large-latest | $2.00 / – / $6.00 | $0.50 / $0.05 / $1.50 |
| gemini-2.5-flash | $0.15 / – / $0.60 | $0.30 / $0.03 / $2.50 |
| gemini-2.5-flash-lite | was charged at the flash price | $0.10 / $0.01 / $0.40 |

• Fourth row fixed: mistral-large-2512 uses the same rule as mistral-large-latest, so it's fixed too, as the plan expected.
• flash-lite: it was being caught by the flash rule, so it now has its own rule placed before it.
• Still wrong: gemini-2.5-flash-image and gemini-2.5-flash-preview-tts were already mismatched and still are, because the flash rule catches them as well.

File size: cost.rs went from 3613 to 3589 lines, under its 3605 cap. Two tests there checked the old prices, so I moved them, names unchanged, into price_audit_tests.rs and updated their expected costs. I also added one test that reads all three new prices through the same lookup /cost uses. When I put the old mistral-large price back, both that test and the moved mistral test failed by name; after restoring it the full suite passes.

Docs: docs/src/features/cost-tracking.md still listed the old prices, so I updated it and added a flash-lite row. It also said there was "no drift detection yet (see #937)", which was false: #937 is closed, and the audit I ran is the result. That line now gives the audit command and points to #1012.

#1012: I posted the before/after audit summaries, the rows fixed, and the next two, as the plan asked. The comment went up before the push, so it cites the two commit IDs as not yet on GitHub. The rows left for later:
• mistral-small-latest needs an exact-match rule.
• deepseek-v4-pro is a recorded known-divergence decision.
